# hashiru.conf — per-machine shell/install variables.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/hashiru/hashiru.conf. The old location — hashiru.conf beside
# this file, inside the checkout — is still read, but loses to ~/.config when
# both exist. Nothing per-machine belongs inside the checkout.
#
# Sourced by lib/common.sh before any default is applied, so use the
# `: "${VAR=value}"` form: it sets the variable only when unset, which keeps
# one-off environment overrides working:
#
#   HASHIRU_UNATTENDED=1 ./install.sh

# Skip every interactive prompt. Set automatically on the ISO's firstboot run;
# set it here if you want manual installs to be non-interactive too.
#: "${HASHIRU_UNATTENDED=1}"

# Keep child-process output (pacman transactions, makepkg builds, git clones)
# off the console and in the log, behind a single progress line. Defaults to
# whatever HASHIRU_UNATTENDED is: on for the ISO's first boot, where the flood
# is the problem and nobody is reading it, and off for manual runs, where that
# output is what you debug against.
#
# Set it to 1 to quiet a manual install, or to 0 to make first boot verbose
# again. `hashiru log` reads the full stream either way.
#: "${HASHIRU_QUIET=1}"

# Skip the mirror ranking at the top of stage 10 — reflector, and about a
# minute of it. That ranking is an argument about *first install*: it happens
# before the heaviest download of the machine's life, on whatever mirrorlist
# archinstall left behind. By the time you are running updates, reflector.timer
# has been re-ranking weekly for months and the foreground run buys nothing.
# Setting this does not disable reflector — the timer is still enabled.
#
# `hashiru update --no-reflector` does the same thing for one run.
#: "${HASHIRU_NO_REFLECTOR=1}"

# This file is only for variables the install scripts read. Machine-local
# *config* — monitors, keybinds, terminal and bar tweaks — goes in the sibling
# directories instead: ~/.config/hashiru/{hypr,kitty,waybar}/. See
# docs/internals.md, "Machine-local overrides".

# There is deliberately nothing here about dotfiles. Hashiru ships the whole
# machine — desktop, terminal, shell, prompt, aliases — and does not clone,
# stow, or read a personal config repo. Keep an editor config of your own if
# you want one on other people's machines; Hashiru has no opinion about it.
